  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Nicknamed "little Corsica", the Pointe des Sardinaux is a natural, wild headland, a peaceful place where you can picnic, sunbathe and observe plants, seaweed, shells and crustaceans. It is reached via the corniche towards Saint-Raphaël, just before La Nartelle beach — the spot stays discreet from the road, access is on foot and the limited parking is along the roadside. You can picnic in the shade of umbrella pines, relax on a small sandy beach at the end of the headland or go for a dip (tourist office). The site preserves two witnesses of the past: a Second World War bunker, a relic of the Provence landings of 15 August 1944, and a fish tank dating from Roman times.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the Sainte-Maxime tourist office, the land part of the Pointe des Sardinaux is one of only two areas of the town's coastline where dogs are accepted year-round — except in July and August, when access is closed to them. The southern tip of the headland, however, remains closed to dogs year-round, as on the rest of the town's beaches. Owners must keep their dog on a leash and pick up its droppings. Owned by the Var département and managed by the town, the headland is part of the coastal path.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: come outside the summer months (before July or after August) to enjoy the site with your dog — in high season, better to head to the <a href="/en/fiche-sainte-maxime">Pont du Préconil dog beach</a>, open year-round without interruption.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>La Nartelle beach is just after (closed to dogs year-round). For a beach in the town centre where your dog is accepted, see the <a href="/en/fiche-sainte-maxime">Pont du Préconil dog beach</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>Practical:</strong> address given by the tourist office: 1 avenue du Général Touzet du Vigier, 83120 Sainte-Maxime; free access year-round, picnic on the grass in the shade of the pines, public toilets and parking nearby, bus stop within 500 m. Assistance dogs are admitted on all beaches. Tourist office: 04 94 55 75 55.</p>
      <p><strong>In July-August:</strong> the town has also created the Jardin des Chiens des Virgiles, a space where dogs roam off leash (except category 2) under their owner's supervision, behind the Virgiles school (access along the school or via the footbridge on the motorhome area side, Neuenbürg roundabout).</p>
      <p><strong>Worth checking before you go:</strong> the town's animal welfare page (2023) does not mention the July-August exception that the tourist office states in 2026; the exact start and end dates of the ban are not published.</p>
      <p>Sources read on 06/11/2026: <a href="https://www.sainte-maxime.com/explorer/la-mer-a-linfini/toutes-les-plages-de-sainte-maxime/la-pointe-des-sardinaux">sainte-maxime.com (Pointe des Sardinaux)</a>, <a href="https://www.sainte-maxime.com/faq/">tourist office FAQ</a>, <a href="https://www.sainte-maxime.fr/ma-ville/cadre-de-vie/developpement-durable/bien-etre-animal/nos-projets-pour-les-animaux">town website</a>.</p>
